  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $5x-10y<-20?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-10y & <-20 \\\\ -10y & <-5x-20 \\\\ 10y & >5x+20 \\\\ y & >\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$ Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y>\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/351bb4b4324432e2cc747e67dac22100da3c9e5318339832efa046f504394c35.png)",
